MF = """<p><b>中文摘要。</b>本著作是一部心理学史，但它不是“流派名录”，而是一部<b>划线史</b>：一百五十年里，心理学家反复在做同一件事——把“心”划出一道线，把什么划进来、把什么划到外面，然后为这条线付账。</p>
<p><b>核心主张。</b>心理学没有“统一”过，也不该被写成通向统一的进步史。每一次流派更替，都是一次<b>六边取舍</b>：它想<b>看见</b>什么（划什么线、用什么区分）、<b>记住</b>什么（哪些材料被保存、哪些被过滤）、<b>跟上</b>什么（用哪一只钟计量）、<b>选择</b>了什么（完备、可组合、可传递三者保二让一）、谁有资格<b>讲述</b>（谁被承认为科学）、以及它的门槛是观察到的还是<b>规定</b>的。每一次划线都有收益，也都有账单；本书逐章清点的是账单。</p>
<p><b>结构与内容。</b>全书六部分二十五章。第一部分“划线的诞生”：从柏拉图的三种灵魂、笛卡尔的灵肉分居、洛克的白板到冯特的实验室，看“心”如何被变成可测量的对象。第二部分“深度的代价”：弗洛伊德把无意识划进来，换来一整套关于遗忘与移情的语言，让出的是可组合性；随后是荣格与阿德勒的分裂、克莱因与温尼科特的客体关系、鲍尔比与安斯沃斯的依恋、拉康的语言转向、神经精神分析与心理测量。第三部分“可观察的约束”：华生与巴甫洛夫、斯金纳的箱子、行为疗法的短程钟、以及行为主义未死的遗产。第四部分“内部的回归”：认知革命、皮亚杰与乔姆斯基的先天后天之争、认知神经科学、社会认知与情境的力量。第五部分“意义与时间”：人本主义、存在主义的时间结构、发展心理学的一生之钟。第六部分“系统与定价”：系统与后现代对“谁有资格定义问题”的追问、CBT 与正念与诊断政治，最后以“六个流派各让出了什么”收束。</p>
<p><b>实证与算例。</b>本书是科普专著，不做第一手实证，但每一处可核对的地方都给了出处与等级：弗洛伊德个案为 C 级、依恋的实验室证据为 B 级、行为强化程序为 A 级；对复现危机（2015 年后）、WEIRD 样本问题与诊断阈值的政治性，逐条写明“我们知道什么、不知道什么”。附录给出流派 × 六核对照表、一百五十年时间线、一百个人名一句话、被埋没与重新发现的清单、可证伪登记簿、数据缺口、分支地图、二十种疗法对照与二十对易混概念。</p>
<p><b>诚实的学术纪律。</b>每一章以一个人物或争议开场，以“它让出了什么”收束；六核不是六个裁判，是六个提问；结论标 A/B/C，结论强度不超过证据强度；书中保留失败与反例（包括“这套读法本身也会漏东西”）。全书结构经 24 条规则的自动校阅：error 0 / warn 0。</p>
<p><b>预测与诊断的边界。</b>本书不裁判疗效，不给临床建议，也不预言心理学的下一次分裂。它只提供一把尺子：下次再听到“这是科学／这不科学”，可以问一句——它划的线在哪，代价落在谁身上。</p>
<p><b>学术特点。</b>完全开源：正文源文件、插图源文件与构建脚本可复现（内容 CC BY 4.0，代码 MIT）；结构统一：每部分有满栏插图扉页与部分小结，每章八段（主提问／场景／导论／机制／表图／反例／小结／一句话带走）；工具：流派对照表、十个实验与十次复现、方法手册、二十对易混概念。</p>
<p><b>Abstract (English).</b> This is a history of psychology, but not a catalogue of schools: it is a history of <b>drawing lines</b>. For a hundred and fifty years psychologists have done one thing over and over — drawing a line across “the mind”, deciding what falls inside and what is left outside, and then paying for that line.</p>
<p><b>Core thesis.</b> Psychology has never been unified, and should not be written as a progress narrative toward unity. Every change of school is a six-sided trade: what it wants to <b>see</b> (which line, which distinction), what it <b>remembers</b> (which material is kept, which filtered), what it <b>keeps up with</b> (which clock it measures by), what it <b>chooses</b> (two of completeness, composability and transferability, giving up the third), who has the authority to <b>tell</b> (who counts as science), and whether its threshold is observed or <b>stipulated</b>. Every line pays; this book itemises the bill, chapter by chapter.</p>
<p><b>Structure.</b> Six parts, twenty-five chapters. (1) The birth of the line: Plato's tripartite soul, Descartes' split, Locke's blank slate, Wundt's laboratory — how the mind became a measurable object. (2) The price of depth: Freud brings the unconscious inside and gains a language of forgetting and transference, giving up composability; then Jung and Adler split, Klein, Winnicott and Bion build object relations, Bowlby and Ainsworth take attachment into the laboratory, Lacan turns to language, and neuropsychoanalysis and psychometrics arrive. (3) The observable constraint: Watson and Pavlov, Skinner's box, behaviour therapy's short clock, and behaviourism's undead legacy. (4) The return of the interior: the cognitive revolution, Piaget versus Chomsky, cognitive neuroscience, social cognition and the power of situation. (5) Meaning and time: humanistic psychology, existential time-structure, developmental psychology's lifelong clock. (6) Systems and pricing: who has the authority to define the problem, CBT and mindfulness and diagnostic politics, closing with what each of six schools gave up.</p>
<p><b>Evidence.</b> A popular-science monograph, it offers no first-hand experiments, but every checkable claim carries a source and a grade: Freud's case studies grade C, attachment's laboratory evidence B, reinforcement schedules A. The replication crisis after 2015, WEIRD samples, and the politics of diagnostic thresholds are stated for what they are. Appendices give a school-by-core table, a 150-year timeline, one hundred names in one sentence each, a register of buried and recovered ideas, a falsifiability ledger, data gaps, a map of subfields, twenty therapies compared, and twenty confusable concept pairs.</p>
<p><b>Discipline.</b> Each chapter opens with a person or a controversy and closes with what the school gave up; the six cores are six questions, not six judges; claims are graded A/B/C and no conclusion is stronger than its evidence; failures and counterexamples are kept. The book passes a 24-rule structural audit with zero errors and zero warnings.</p>
<p><b>Boundaries.</b> It does not adjudicate efficacy, offers no clinical advice, and does not predict psychology's next split. It offers one ruler: next time someone says “this is science”, ask where the line was drawn and who paid for it.</p>
<p><b>Features.</b> Fully open source (content CC BY 4.0, code MIT), with manuscript, figures and build scripts reproducible on GitHub; uniform structure — a full-page illustrated plate and a part summary for each part, and an eight-part skeleton for each chapter; tools include the school comparison table, ten experiments and ten replications, a methods handbook, and twenty confusable pairs.</p>"""
